 The vectors $\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ - 1 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix},$ $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 4 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix},$ and $\begin{pmatrix} a \\ 1 \\ c \end{pmatrix}$ are pairwise orthogonal, so their dot products are zero. The dot product of the first and second vectors is \(1 \cdot 2 + (-1) \cdot 4 + 2 \cdot 1 = 2 - 4 + 2 = 0\). The dot product of the first and third vectors is \(1 \cdot a + (-1) \cdot 1 + 2 \cdot c = a - 1 + 2c = 0\). The dot product of the second and third vectors is \(2 \cdot a + 4 \cdot 1 + 1 \cdot c = 2a + 4 + c = 0\). These equations give us the system of equations: \[
\begin{cases}
a + 2c = 1 \\
2a + c = -4
\end{cases}
\] Solving these equations, we get \(a = -2\) and \(c = \frac{3}{2}\). Therefore, the ordered pair \((a, c)\) is \((-2, \frac{3}{2})\). </think> <answer> \((-2, \frac{3}{2})\) </answer>

